  abstract = {BACKGROUND: The risk of cancer with hypercalcaemia in primary care is unknown. METHODS: This was a cohort study using calcium results in patients aged ⩾40 years in a primary care electronic data set. Diagnoses of cancer in the following year were identified. RESULTS: Participants (54 267) had calcium results: 1674 (3\%) were ⩾2.6 mmol l(-1). Hypercalcaemia was strongly associated with cancer, especially in males: OR 2.92, 95\% CI 2.17-3.93, P={\textexclamdown}0.001; positive predictive value (PPV) 11.5\%; females: OR 1.86, 95\% CI 1.39-2.50, P{\textexclamdown}0.001: PPV 4.1\%. CONCLUSIONS: Hypercalcaemia is strongly associated with cancer in primary care, with men at most risk, despite hypercalcaemia being more common in women.},
