pisodes was 142.8 (95\% CI, 127.4-160.1) and 84.4 (95\% CI, 75.4-94.5) days, respectively, with a difference in means of 58.4 (95\% CI, 39.4-77.4; P {$<$} .001). Between baseline and 12 months, participants in the water group, compared with those in the control group, had increased mean (SD) urine volume (1.4 [0.04] vs 0.1 [0.04] L; P {$<$} .001) and voids (2.4 [0.2] vs -0.1 [0.2]; P {$<$} .001) and decreased urine osmolality (-402.8 [19.6] vs -24.0 [19.5] mOsm/kg; P {$<$} .001). Conclusions and Relevance: Increased water intake is an effective antimicrobial-sparing strategy to prevent recurrent cystitis in premenopausal women at high risk for recurrence who drink low volumes of fluid daily. Trial Registration: ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02444975.},
