Malarstig, Anders and Mannermaa, Arto and Maranville, Joseph and Matakidou, Athena and Meretoja, Tuomo and Mozaffari, Sahar V and Niemi, Mari E K and Niemi, Marianna and Niiranen, Teemu and O Donnell, Christopher J and Obeidat, Ma En and Okafo, George and Ollila, Hanna M and Palom{\"a}ki, Antti and Palotie, Tuula and Partanen, Jukka and Paul, Dirk S and Pelkonen, Margit and Pendergrass, Rion K and Petrovski, Slav{\'e} and Pitk{\"a}ranta, Anne and Platt, Adam and Pulford, David and Punkka, Eero and Pussinen, Pirkko and Raghavan, Neha and Rahimov, Fedik and Rajpal, Deepak and Renaud, Nicole A and {Riley-Gillis}, Bridget and Rodosthenous, Rodosthenis and Saarentaus, Elmo and Salminen, Aino and Salminen, Eveliina and Salomaa, Veikko and Schleutker, Johanna and Serpi, Raisa and Shen, Huei-Yi and Siegel, Richard and Silander, Kaisa and Siltanen, Sanna and Soini, Sirpa and Soininen, Hilkka and Sul, Jae Hoon and Tachmazidou, Ioanna and Tasanen, Kaisa and Tienari, Pentti and {Toppila-Salmi}, Sanna and Tukiainen, Taru and Tuomi, Tiinamaija and Turunen, Joni A and Ulirsch, Jacob C and Vaura, Felix and Virolainen, Petri and Waring, Jeffrey and Waterworth, Dawn and Yang, Robert and Nelis, Mari and Reigo, Anu and Metspalu, Andres and Milani, Lili and Esko, T{\~o}nu and Fox, Caroline and Havulinna, Aki S and Perola, Markus and Ripatti, Samuli and Jalanko, Anu and Laitinen, Tarja and M{\"a}kel{\"a}, Tomi P and Plenge, Robert and McCarthy, Mark and Runz, Heiko and Daly, Mark J and Palotie, Aarno},
  year = {2023},
  month = jan,
  journal = {Nature},
  volume = {613},
  number = {7944},
  pages = {508--518},
  publisher = {{Springer Science and Business Media LLC}},
  abstract = {Population isolates such as those in Finland benefit genetic research because deleterious alleles are often concentrated on a small number of low-frequency variants (0.1\% {\textbackslash}leq minor allele frequency {$<$} 5\%). These variants survived the founding bottleneck rather than being distributed over a large number of ultrarare variants. Although this effect is well established in Mendelian genetics, its value in common disease genetics is less explored1,2. FinnGen aims to study the genome and national health register data of 500,000 Finnish individuals. Given the relatively high median age of participants (63 years) and the substantial fraction of hospital-based recruitment, FinnGen is enriched for disease end points. Here we analyse data from 224,737 participants from FinnGen and study 15 diseases that have previously been investigated in large genome-wide association studies (GWASs). We also include meta-analyses of biobank data from Estonia and the United Kingdom. We identified 30 new associations, primarily low-frequency variants, enriched in the Finnish population. A GWAS of 1,932 diseases also identified 2,733 genome-wide significant associations (893 phenome-wide significant (PWS), P {$<$} 2.6 {\textbackslash}times 10-11) at 2,496 (771 PWS) independent loci with 807 (247 PWS) end points. Among these, fine-mapping implicated 148 (73 PWS) coding variants associated with 83 (42 PWS) end points. Moreover, 91 (47 PWS) had an allele frequency of {$<$}5\% in non-Finnish European individuals, of which 62 (32 PWS) were enriched by more than twofold in Finland. These findings demonstrate the power of bottlenecked populations to find entry points into the biology of common diseases through low-frequency, high impact variants.},
  copyright = {https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0},
  langid = {english}
}

