d Matakidou, Athena and Meretoja, Tuomo and Mozaffari, Sahar V and Niemi, Mari E K and Niemi, Marianna and Niiranen, Teemu and O'Donnell, Christopher J and Obeidat, Ma'en and Okafo, George and Ollila, Hanna M and Palom{\"a}ki, Antti and Palotie, Tuula and Partanen, Jukka and Paul, Dirk S and Pelkonen, Margit and Pendergrass, Rion K and Petrovski, Slav{\'e} and Pitk{\"a}ranta, Anne and Platt, Adam and Pulford, David and Punkka, Eero and Pussinen, Pirkko and Raghavan, Neha and Rahimov, Fedik and Rajpal, Deepak and Renaud, Nicole A and {Riley-Gillis}, Bridget and Rodosthenous, Rodosthenis and Saarentaus, Elmo and Salminen, Aino and Salminen, Eveliina and Salomaa, Veikko and Schleutker, Johanna and Serpi, Raisa and Shen, Huei-Yi and Siegel, Richard and Silander, Kaisa and Siltanen, Sanna and Soini, Sirpa and Soininen, Hilkka and Sul, Jae H and Tachmazidou, Ioanna and Tasanen, Kaisa and Tienari, Pentti and {Toppila-Salmi}, Sanna and Tukiainen, Taru and Tuomi, Tiinamaija and Turunen, Joni A and Ulirsch, Jacob C and Vaura, Felix and Virolainen, Petri and Waring, Jeffrey and Waterworth, Dawn and Yang, Robert and Nelis, Mari and Reigo, Anu and Metspalu, Andres and Milani, Lili and Esko, T{\~o}nu and Fox, Caroline and Havulinna, Aki S and Perola, Markus and Ripatti, Samuli and Jalanko, Anu and Laitinen, Tarja and M{\"a}kel{\"a}, Tomi and Plenge, Robert and McCarthy, Mark and Runz, Heiko and Daly, Mark J and Palotie, Aarno},
  year = {2022},
  month = mar,
  journal = {bioRxiv},
  abstract = {ABSTRACTPopulation isolates such as Finland provide benefits in genetic studies because the allelic spectrum of damaging alleles in any gene is often concentrated on a small number of low-frequency variants (0.1\% {\textbackslash}leq minor allele frequency We studied a benchmark set of 15 diseases that had previously been investigated in large genome-wide association studies. FinnGen discovery analyses were meta-analysed in Estonian and UK biobanks. We identify 30 novel associations, primarily low-frequency variants strongly enriched, in or specific to, the Finnish population and Uralic language family neighbors in Estonia and Russia.These findings demonstrate the power of bottlenecked populations to find unique entry points into the biology of common diseases through low-frequency, high impact variants. Such high impact variants have a potential to contribute to medical translation including drug discovery.},
