 The Tenckhoff catheter was removed and she was converted to haemodialysis using a dialysate bath containing 3.5 mEq/L of calcium. Oral calcium supplements were ceased. A month after treatment, the serum calcium was 2.33 mmol/L, the parathyroid hormone level was 644 pmol/L and the 1,25 (OH)2 Vit D3 was 12 pg/mL (16.4-42.4 pg/mL) (Figure 1). Hypercalcaemia is a known complication of active tuberculosis and is believed to be secondary to extrarenal formation of 1,25 (OH)2 Vit D3 by the macrophages of tuberculous granulomata (1). In our patient, the serum calcium was elevated at the onset of tuberculous peritonitis although it had been normal over the preceeding six months when she was taking calcium carbonate as a phosphate binder. With the elevation of serum calcium, the parathyroid hormone level was depressed. Her initial 1,25 (OH)2 Vit D3 level was inappropriately high for her underlying end-stage renal disease and calcium level. Both the parathyroid hormone and the 1,25 (OH)2 Vit D3 level returned to levels consistent with end-stage renal failure after treatment of her tuberculosis. As such, we believe that the tuberculosis peritonitis caused increased production of 1,25 (OH)2 Vit D3, which together with hypercalcaemia suppressed parathyroid hormone production. Tuberculous peritonitis is uncommon in CAPD patients. With the increasing number of CAPD patients in areas where tuberculosis is prevalent, the possibility of tuberculous peritonitis as a cause of hypercalcaemia in these patients must be considered (3).},
